<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deuteronomistic History</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Deuteronomistic History is the name scholars give to the books of Joshua, Judges, Samuel and Kings, read as a single historical work shaped by the language and theology of Deuteronomy. Proposed by Martin Noth in 1943, the theory holds that it was compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> in the late 7th century BCE, probably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a>, and revised during the Babylonian exile. It explains the fall of Israel and Judah as punishment for unfaithfulness to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a>.</p></div>
